T2.2 logic kernels on the REFERENCE DEVICE -- X-6's raw log, plus an arbitration
===============================================================================

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Two committed measurements of the same kernel on the same
device disagreed by ~1.5x and neither entry said so:

  EXPERIMENTS.md X-6   bitwiseAnd uint32 640x480   0.02240 ns/px   16.74 GB/s
  EXPERIMENTS.md X-9   bitwiseAnd uint32 640x480   0.0327  ns/px   11.48 GB/s
  EXPERIMENTS.md X-10  bitwiseAnd uint32 640x480   0.0328  ns/px   11.43 GB/s

Same device, same governor, same core pinning, same compiler. X-6 also had no
committed raw log -- results/logic_benchmark.log holds only the x86_64 run -- so
the disagreement could not be arbitrated from inside the repository. This run
fixes both halves: it is a reference-device run of the same benchmark X-6 used,
and it settles which number belongs to what.

THE ANSWER: BOTH ARE RIGHT, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE FIXTURE, NOT THE KERNEL.

  logic_benchmark      holds ONE image triple      115200 B resident
  alignment/wordwidth  hold 4 input pairs + dst    345600 B resident per variant,
                       and interleave 4 variants   ~1.3-1.6 MiB per round

A 115 KiB working set on a Cortex-A72 streams largely out of L1/L2; a 337 KiB one
streams out of L2 only and is partly evicted between round-robin batches. X-6's
OWN size sweep predicts the gap without any new assumption:

  X-6 working-set sweep    256x256    8 KiB x3    0.05064 ns/px
                           512x512   32 KiB x3    0.02997 ns/px
                          1024x1024 128 KiB x3    0.03116 ns/px   <- ~384 KiB total
                          2048x2048 512 KiB x3    0.06475 ns/px

At a ~384 KiB working set X-6 measured 0.031 ns/px. The alignment and wordwidth
benchmarks hold ~338 KiB and measure 0.0327-0.0330. That is the same machine
behaving the same way, and it means neither entry is wrong -- but their ABSOLUTE
ns/px are not comparable with each other, which is now stated in all three.

Nothing about this changes X-9 or X-10: both are ratios between variants that
share one fixture, so the fixture cancels.

CAVEAT ON READING THIS AS "X-6 REPRODUCED". It is a corroboration run, not a
re-issue of X-6's numbers. The device now has OpenCV 4.10.0, where X-6 ran
against a different install, so the DENOMINATOR moved and the ratios below are
not X-6's to the digit -- bitwiseAnd is 27.95x here against X-6's 29.1x, and
bitwiseNot is 19.48x against X-6's 17.3x. X-6's headline claim, ~28-30x on the
target for and/or/xor, stands. The binCV side is what this run was taken for, and
that side reproduces to within 5%: 0.02350 ns/px against 0.02240.

PLATFORM

  device:           pi4
  cpu:              Raspberry Pi 4 Model B Rev 1.5 (Cortex-A72)
  arch / kernel:    aarch64 / 6.18.34+rpt-rpi-v8
  compiler:         g++ (Debian 14.2.0-19) 14.2.0, Release (-O2 -DNDEBUG)
  opencv:           4.10.0, cv::getNumThreads() = 1 (taskset pins one core)
  governor:         performance (restored to ondemand on exit)
  core pinning:     taskset -c 3
  throttled before / after: throttled=0x0 / throttled=0x0
  commit:           3f32493

X-7 CAVEAT does not bite here: these are logic kernels, not reductions, so no
popcount is issued. No -march flag was added.

Full output follows.

